#use(std/code)
#use(std/footnote)

#section{Footnotes}::

#code{#use(std/footnote)} provides #code|{#note{…}}| — a numbered superscript
marker whose body is recorded at collect time — and #code{#notes}, which
renders the numbered list with backlinks. Two-pass: a #code{#note} placed
after #code{#notes} still appears in the list, in document order

#code(lang: "plain")
	First claim.#note{A supporting detail.} Second claim.#note{Another detail.}

	#notes

Live:

First claim.#note{A supporting detail.} Second claim.#note{Another detail.}

#notes

Corner cases:

#list
	#item: Numbering is document order, allocated at collect time — markers read 1, 2, 3 … as they appear in the source
	#item
		#code{#notes} may appear #strong{once}: a second one would duplicate every note's element ids, so it is rejected
		with an explicit error. In this shared-info how-to set that means once across the whole set
	#item: Zero notes is fine: #code{#notes} then renders an empty list, like an empty glossary
	#item: Note bodies are full markup and may contain #code{#ref}s or other calls
